<div id="ops" class="visually-hidden" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<section id="fleet" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Fleet</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Digital Technology console — computers, live util, sessions, online status, storage, client version, cost, and stats in one place.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Open <a asp-page="/Fleet">Fleet</a>. Tabs load on demand (only the active tab’s data runs on first paint).</li>
        <li>Legacy URLs (<code>/</code>, <code>/Ops</code>, Sessions, Online Status, Client Version, Cost, Stats) redirect into the matching Fleet tab.</li>
        <li><strong>Live</strong> shows estate-wide 30s util gauges for every known machine (not Flood-gated).</li>
        <li><strong>Storage</strong> shows drive free-space bars and weekly deep-scan results (top folders / large files / hotspots).</li>
        <li>Online Status still uses on-demand Ping / Test RDP — probes are not run on every page load.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="storage" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Fleet → Storage</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> central disk-space view — per-machine volume fill, last deep scan, and known hotspots (<code>ccmcache</code>, <code>Projects</code>, user profiles).</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Open <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="storage">Fleet → Storage</a>. Cards show drive used/free with green (&lt;70%), amber (70–85%), red (&gt;85%) bars from heartbeat volume data.</li>
        <li>Click a card to expand top non-system folders, large files, and hotspots from the last deep scan.</li>
        <li>The API queues a weekly fleet scan (<code>Heimdall:StorageScan</code> — day/time UTC, min agent version; <code>RootPath</code> <code>*</code> = all fixed drives). <strong>Run scan now</strong> queues all eligible hosts; expand a card for a single host. Offline agents keep the pending request until they check in.</li>
        <li>Fleet scans use agent disk usage (≥ v6): all fixed drives, top 5 non-system folders, top 5 files ≥ 1&nbsp;GB, plus hotspot sizes. Per-host custom scans remain on the machine detail Storage panel (drive picker + min file size with MB/GB/TB).</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="fleet-live" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Fleet → Live</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> live CPU/GPU/RAM/disk/network for the whole estate — every machine with a Heimdall client.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Open <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="live">Fleet → Live</a>. Auto-refreshes every 30s.</li>
        <li>Sampling is always-on after the agent’s first heartbeat — no Flood enrollment required.</li>
        <li>Orange halo on the machine name means no agent contact in ~60 seconds (<code>LastSeenUtc</code> / fleet snapshot or heartbeat). After a shutdown, expect offline within about two missed 30s samples.</li>
        <li>Active / Idle badges apply while TUFLOW is detected on that host; most estate machines show gauges with Active/Idle as —.</li>
        <li>Flood’s own Live tab (under <a asp-page="/Flood">Flood hub</a>) stays scoped to the Flood allowlist.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="machines" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Computers</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> fleet list by team — status and utilisation at a glance (Fleet → All computers).</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Computers are grouped under <strong>team section headings</strong>. Active app-list names for that team appear beside the heading.</li>
        <li>Summary counts (Computers / Active / Idle / Off) sit beside the Period control — Computers in gold; Active / Idle / Off labels in status colours (green / amber / red); silver numbers centered underneath. Click any column header to sort (first click descending, then toggle).</li>
        <li><strong>Status</strong> (~60s check-in): green Active = online with a non-disconnected session; amber Idle = online, no active session; red Off = no recent check-in. Status is colored text (same size as the computer name).</li>
        <li><strong>Period</strong> (Today / 24h / 5d / 7d / 30d / All) sets Active / Passive / Free % and GPU·CPU·IO columns for the whole list.</li>
        <li>Click any metric cell (Active, Passive, Free, GPU h, CPU h, Dr, Dw, NTx, NRx) to open a <strong>breakdown</strong> of processes, people, and days that built that total. Period cycling on cells was removed — change Period only via the dropdown.</li>
        <li>Active % is WTS Active (connected) session time over the calendar window (agent ActiveSeconds; Disconnected time is excluded; stale open sessions stop at last observation). Passive, GPU/CPU hours, and disk/network columns use <strong>always-on 30s fleet samples</strong> from every Heimdall client (independent of Flood enrollment). Until samples arrive those cells show —.</li>
        <li>Process names on hardware/Passive breakdowns require an <strong>updated agent</strong> that uploads top processes with each fleet sample. Older clients still contribute machine totals; the drill-down notes when process history is missing.</li>
        <li>Name links to computer detail. Assign teams and app lists under <a asp-page="/Teams">Applications → Teams</a>.</li>
        <li>Sessions live under <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="sessions">Fleet → Sessions</a>.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="applications" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Application Usage</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> fleet-wide usage for tracked processes — time, machines, users, and runs over the selected period.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Pick a <strong>Period</strong> and Apply. Columns: Users, Machines, Avg open, Runs, Total time, Last seen.</li>
        <li>Click a row for per-machine / per-user detail. Long names truncate; hover for the full title.</li>
        <li>To change <em>what</em> counts as an application (vs. Core Windows / SOE noise), use <a asp-page="/AppLists" asp-fragment="app-lists">App lists</a> or <a asp-page="/Discovery" asp-fragment="discovery">Discovery &amp; Classification</a>.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="app-lists" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">App lists</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> the only place to build and apply lists of processes agents should track.
        Tabs (Fleet-style): <strong>Software Lists</strong>, <strong>Create</strong>, <strong>Analyze PC</strong>.
    </p>
    <ol class="small">
        <li><strong>Software Lists</strong> — browse/edit/export existing lists, plus <em>Track Software</em> (apply a list to Global / regions / offices / machines). Team apply/ignore also lives under <a asp-page="/Teams">Teams</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Create</strong> — compact Name / optional Team / Notes. Pick apps from the catalog table (Discovery-style) with <em>Add to list</em>, then <em>Save</em> (requires a name). Use <em>Add custom software to catalog</em> for path/exe/friendly name/description before the app is installed anywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Analyze PC</strong> — machine lookup, inventory, team-apps hunting, bulk classification import/export.</li>
        <li>Agents only track processes that appear on an applied App list (plus any leftover legacy Tracking Config includes). There is no separate Known Apps track list.</li>
        <li><strong>Buttons</strong> — Gold = primary; Silver = secondary; Black = nav; Rose = cancel/remove.</li>
        <li><strong>Changelog</strong> opens the full <a asp-page="/AppListChangelog">App list changelog</a>.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="app-list-changelog" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">App list changelog</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> full audit trail for app-list work — list create/edit, uploads, assignments, machine analysis approvals, and classification imports.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Open from <em>View Changelog</em> on <a asp-page="/AppLists" asp-fragment="app-lists">App lists</a> (or go directly to <a asp-page="/AppListChangelog">/AppListChangelog</a>).</li>
        <li>Newest entries first; 50 rows per page with Previous / Next when needed.</li>
        <li>Click column headers to sort within the current page.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="discovery" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Discovery &amp; Classification</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> classify processes into Core Windows / SOE / Specialization. By default only unclassified apps
        and pending suggestions (from CSV import or heuristics) are listed.
    </p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Name &amp; Location</strong> — process name (double-click to rename) and read-only path underneath. Click <em>Name</em> or <em>Location</em> in the header to sort (no arrow indicators). Drag column borders to resize; widths persist for the browser session.</li>
        <li><strong>Version</strong> — file/product/manual/path-derived value (no source subtitle).</li>
        <li><strong>Category</strong> / <strong>Subcategory</strong> — from CSV import or Edit/Save; shown when present (not always “—”).</li>
        <li><strong>Classification</strong> — in-row dropdown (Core Windows / SOE / Specialization). Click <strong>Set</strong> to apply that group (and current Category/Subcategory).</li>
        <li><strong>Advertise RDP</strong> — checkbox (and batch Advertise on/off) lists the app on the public Remote Workstations pool. Specialization Set/Approve opts in; uncheck to clean the list (moving to SOE does not auto-clear).</li>
        <li><strong>Installs</strong> — machines that reported the process; hover for the host list.</li>
        <li><strong>Approve</strong> (gold) — commits a pending Group suggestion. <strong>Set</strong> uses the Classification dropdown instead of a modal.</li>
        <li><strong>Search</strong> — type a filter and press Search or Enter (not live-as-you-type).</li>
        <li><strong>Missing path</strong> — catalog rows with an empty ExecutablePath (shown as “missing path”). Use <strong>Find missing paths</strong> (or per-row <strong>Find path</strong>) to reuse paths from process samples / machine inventories; remaining gaps can trigger Request Inventory on those hosts. Or request inventory manually on App Lists.</li>
        <li><strong>Show classified</strong> — expand the list beyond unclassified / pending suggestions. <strong>Descriptions</strong> toggle shows description sub-rows.</li>
        <li>Import classifications from <a asp-page="/AppLists" asp-fragment="csv-classifications">App lists → Bulk Update</a>; they appear here as pending until Approve/Set.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="socratize" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Socratize</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> a one-machine cost-justification brief — probes sessions, local vs. RDP time, utilisation, apps, and team, then gives a verdict.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Pick (or search for) a machine.</li>
        <li>Review the generated brief — utilisation score, busy metrics, apps in use, and a keep/investigate/retire-style verdict.</li>
        <li>The verdict's thresholds come from <a asp-page="/Utilization" asp-fragment="utilization">Utilization criteria</a> — tune those if verdicts feel off.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="sessions" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Sessions</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> logon / usage sessions — who signed in, local vs RDP, start/end, active vs disconnected time. Under <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="sessions">Fleet → Sessions</a>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Pick a <strong>Period</strong> (Today / 24h / 5d / 7d / 30d / All). Location filter (Country → City) narrows the machine list when set.</li>
        <li>Click a machine name or multi-select to open the session log. Duration cells are clickable where drill-down is available.</li>
        <li>Each row shows user, Local / Inbound RDP, started, ended, total duration, active time, and disconnected time.</li>
        <li>Open-count buttons show who is on a machine right now; <em>Machine detail</em> links to the apps/metrics page for that host.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="remote-machines" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Online Status</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Ping, RDP handshake, and RDS service from this API host — also under <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="online">Fleet → Online status</a>. Utilisation lives on Computers; live metrics on Live.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Online / Offline</strong> means the Heimdall agent last contacted the API within ~60 seconds (<code>LastSeenUtc</code> / <code>RemoteMachineService.OnlineWindow</code>) — not ICMP ping and not RDP. Fleet snapshots (~30s) and heartbeats both refresh contact; after shutdown, offline appears after about two missed samples. A machine can be Offline here while Ping succeeds and RDP accepts (agent stopped, wrong API URL/key, or ingest not reaching this DB). Client Version keeps a separate 5‑minute Online window for the deploy queue.</li>
        <li><strong>Ping</strong> uses ICMP from this API host (may be blocked by firewall). Click Ping to probe — results are not automatic.</li>
        <li><strong>RDS service</strong> status is reported by the agent on each heartbeat.</li>
        <li><strong>Restart RDS</strong> queues <code>RestartTermService</code> for the agent. A per-machine countdown estimates agent pickup (~config refresh interval, typically 300s). When the agent acknowledges the restart (or TermService reports Running after the expected window), the API automatically runs an RDP test and updates the row.</li>
        <li><strong>RDP probe</strong> opens TCP 3389 from the API server and validates the RDP negotiation handshake (no WinRM required).</li>
        <li><strong>Connect</strong> opens an <code>.rdp</code> to the agent-reported IP when known, otherwise the hostname.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="tuflow-runs" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">TUFLOW Runs</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> queue a TUFLOW start or graceful stop on Flood-enrolled machines.
        Nav under <strong>Flood → TUFLOW Runs</strong> (visible only to <code>Heimdall:StaffAccess:AdminEmails</code> ∪ <code>Heimdall:FloodTeamEmails</code>).
    </p>
    <ol class="small">
        <li>Enroll the modelling host on <strong>Flood → Enrollment</strong> first (Flood allowlist — separate from util sampling, which is always-on for all clients).</li>
        <li>On <strong>TUFLOW Runs</strong>, choose <strong>TUFLOW exe + .tcf</strong> or <strong>Use existing CMD</strong>. Paths must be a local volume on the host (<code>C:\</code>–<code>F:\</code>) or <strong>UNC</strong> (<code>\\server\share\…</code>). Mapped drive letters (<code>P:\</code>, <code>S:\</code>, …) are rejected: they exist only in the logged-on user’s session, and HeimdallAgent launches TUFLOW from a service with nobody logged on. For CMD mode, point at a <code>.cmd</code>/<code>.bat</code> the service account can read; Heimdall validates it looks like a TUFLOW launch, then runs it with <code>cmd.exe /c</code>. Optional scenarios/events/run name apply to exe+.tcf mode. The agent picks up within ~20s.</li>
        <li><strong>Stop gracefully</strong> asks the launcher to send a process-group break so TUFLOW can finish the current checkpoint (resume-friendly if your <code>.tcf</code> writes restart files — see <code>tuflow-automation/tcf/</code>). Prefer scripts that call TUFLOW synchronously (not <code>start …</code>) so Heimdall can track/stop the process group.</li>
        <li>Progress comes from TUFLOW’s own <code>.tsf</code>; crashes from <code>.tlf</code> ERROR lines. Machine detail also shows a TUFLOW panel + recent run history for flood-gated users.</li>
        <li>Agent needs <code>%ProgramFiles%\Heimdall\Agent\TuflowLauncher\TuflowLauncher.exe</code> (included when you pack/install the client or run <code>Install-Agent</code>).</li>
        <li>For active-run progress across the fleet, use <a href="#fleet-sims">Fleet Sims</a> (<a asp-page="/Flood" asp-route-tab="sims">Flood → Fleet Sims</a>).</li>
        <li>For start-hour / duration analytics by user and machine, use <a href="#tuflow-behaviour">Run behaviour</a> (<a asp-page="/Flood" asp-route-tab="behaviour">Flood → Run behaviour</a>).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-0">
        Paths are free-form (not allowlisted job definitions yet). Modelling+DT RBAC is a follow-up.
        Always convert mapped letters to UNC (in Explorer: right-click the share → Properties, or <code>net use</code> to see the <code>\\server\share</code>).
    </p>
</section>

<section id="tuflow-behaviour" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">TUFLOW Run Behaviour (Flood → Run behaviour)</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> analytics on when TUFLOW runs usually start and how long they run, by user and by machine —
        built from the same <code>TuflowBehaviourRuns</code> start/end detection that powers Live Active stamps.
    </p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Open <a asp-page="/Flood" asp-route-tab="behaviour">Flood → Run behaviour</a> (or follow the link from <a asp-page="/TuflowRuns">TUFLOW Runs</a>). Default window is last 30 days (7 / 14 / 90 / 180 selectable).</li>
        <li>Click a <strong>Starts</strong> or <strong>Completed</strong> count in the By user / By machine tables to filter the Runs card; or use the Runs toolbar (user, machine, date range, status, show N).</li>
        <li>Use <strong>Hide all charts</strong> / <strong>Show all charts</strong> on the Runs card, or each run’s <strong>Hide chart</strong> / <strong>Show chart</strong> for a single chart.</li>
        <li>Only runs with a confirmed <code>DetectedStartUtc</code> are included. Watching-only (process seen, not yet elevated) are excluded.</li>
        <li>Duration averages / medians use completed runs (<code>DetectedEndUtc</code> set). Open Active runs appear in start counts and the hour-of-day chart but not in duration averages.</li>
        <li>Username comes from the interactive session on the host during sampling (ops.* is not preferred once a non-ops user is known).</li>
        <li>The <strong>Runs</strong> list lists individual confirmed starts (newest first, capped) with avg/peak GPU, time at ≥50% GPU, and a zoomable multi-metric chart (GPU/CPU/RAM% left axis; Disk W / Net Tx MB/s right). Toggle series with checkboxes (all on by default). Older samples may only have GPU/CPU until disk/net/ram fields started filling in.</li>
        <li><strong>Peak GPU</strong> is the highest TUFLOW <em>process</em> GPU% sample in the run. Windows GPU Engine counters are summed across engines/adapters, so Peak can exceed 100% (e.g. 600%+) on multi-engine hosts; the GPU line is drawn clamped to 0–100%. Hover the Peak/Avg/High GPU cells for the formula.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="tuflow-queue" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Run Queue (Flood → Run Queue)</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> spread a batch of scenario/event combos across Flood machines the way a
        <code>.cmd</code> would loop <code>-s1/-e1…</code> with a local instance limit — except idle hosts claim the next job.
    </p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Enroll modelling hosts on <strong>Flood → Enrollment</strong>.</li>
        <li>Open <a asp-page="/Flood" asp-route-tab="queue">Flood → Run Queue</a>. Choose <strong>Fleet</strong> (any idle host) or pin to one machine.</li>
        <li>Enter a local or UNC TUFLOW <code>.exe</code> and a UNC <code>.tcf</code> (mapped <code>P:\</code> is rejected — the agent service cannot see it when logged off).</li>
        <li>Fill <code>-s1…-s4</code> and <code>-e1…-e4</code> groups (space/comma separated within a group). Heimdall builds the cartesian product (same as many batch files). Cap 500 sims per add.</li>
        <li>Each Flood host runs <strong>one Heimdall-launched sim at a time</strong>. When it finishes, the ~20s TUFLOW poll claims the next waiting item (pinned first, then unassigned fleet).</li>
        <li>Watch progress on <a asp-page="/Flood" asp-route-tab="sims">Fleet Sims</a>. Graceful stop remains on <a asp-page="/TuflowRuns">TUFLOW Runs</a>.</li>
        <li>Do not paste a batch that uses <code>start …</code> and mapped drives. Prefer exe+.tcf matrix here so Heimdall can track and stop the process.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="fleet-sims" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Fleet Sims (Flood → Fleet Sims)</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> live progress table for TUFLOW runs currently active on Flood-enrolled machines — complements <a href="#tuflow-runs">TUFLOW Runs</a> and <a href="#tuflow-queue">Run Queue</a>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Open <a asp-page="/Flood" asp-route-tab="sims">Flood → Fleet Sims</a>. Empty when no run is active.</li>
        <li>Columns include run name, machine, started-by, state, progress %, elapsed, estimated remaining, and resource averages since run start.</li>
        <li><strong>TUFLOW process averages</strong> (CPU/GPU/disk R/W) come from 30s fleet snapshots scoped to the TUFLOW process. <strong>Whole-machine</strong> averages include network (no per-process network counter).</li>
        <li>Queue waiting/dispatching counts link to <a href="#tuflow-queue">Run Queue</a>. Start one-offs from <a asp-page="/TuflowRuns">TUFLOW Runs</a>.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="historical-dashboard" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Flood hub (Live / Historical / Enrollment / Sims / Run behaviour)</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> Flood-gated workspace for TUFLOW fleet ops — Live and Historical analytics scoped to the Flood allowlist, Enrollment, Run Queue, Fleet Sims, and Run behaviour.
        Open <a asp-page="/Flood">Flood</a>.
        <strong>Full Flood</strong> = site admins (<code>Heimdall:StaffAccess:AdminEmails</code>) ∪ Full Flood list
        (appsettings <code>FloodTeamEmails</code>, or Admin → <a asp-page="/AccessLists">Access lists</a> DB override).
        <strong>Live-only</strong> = also the Flood Live list (<code>FloodLiveEmails</code> / Access lists).
        Manage team emails under <a asp-page="/AccessLists">Admin → Access lists</a> (applies immediately; no restart).
        Legacy <code>/HistoricalDashboard</code>, <code>/FleetSimProgress</code>, and <code>/TuflowBehaviour</code> URLs redirect into this hub.
        Estate-wide live util (all clients) lives under <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="live">Fleet → Live</a>.
    </p>
    <ol class="small">
        <li><strong>Enrollment</strong> — Flood allowlist for TUFLOW Runs / sims / Flood-scoped analytics. Search by hostname or last-known IP. <em>Util sampling does not require enrollment</em> — every heartbeating client gets 30s fleet snapshots. Full Flood only.</li>
        <li><strong>Live</strong> — latest snapshot on enrolled Flood machines: gauges, TUFLOW status (Active / Idle / Not Running), today’s runtime/active/GPU hours,
            plus optional <strong>CodeMeter HPC/Classic</strong> seats (when <code>Heimdall:CodeMeter:Enabled</code> is on the API host with CodeMeter Runtime).
            Updates via a <strong>shared server stream</strong> (SSE ≈ every 30s): many browsers share one rebuild — they do not each re-query Live.
            Active machines also get a live multi-metric chart (GPU/CPU/RAM% and Disk W / Net Tx; process metrics when present). Open Live to the team via Access lists (Flood Live only).</li>
        <li><strong>Active vs Idle</strong> — while TUFLOW is running: Active if <em>TUFLOW process</em> GPU &gt; 5%, CPU &gt; 10%, or disk read/write &gt; 5 MB/s; otherwise Idle. Not Running when TUFLOW is not detected.</li>
        <li><strong>Historical</strong> — fleet summary cards for enrolled machines, Totals (today / week / month / 30d / 3m / 6m / year) or Averages (daily / 7-day / monthly / all-time), plus rankings (GPU, CPU, RAM, disk I/O, most active). Full Flood only.</li>
        <li><strong>Run Queue</strong> — see <a href="#tuflow-queue">Run Queue</a>. Full Flood only.</li>
        <li><strong>Fleet Sims</strong> — see <a href="#fleet-sims">Fleet Sims</a>. Full Flood only.</li>
        <li><strong>Run behaviour</strong> — see <a href="#tuflow-behaviour">TUFLOW Run Behaviour</a>. Full Flood only.</li>
        <li><strong>Machine detail</strong> — click a hostname (or open <code>/HistoricalDashboardMachine/{id}</code>) for period summary cards and Chart.js time series (24h / 7d / 30d / 90d / 365d). Full Flood only (Live-only viewers see machine names without the detail link).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-0">
        <strong>Retention:</strong> raw 30s samples are purged after ~90 days by default (<code>Heimdall:FleetSnapshotRetention</code>).
        Rough disk estimate: about <em>N × 2,880</em> rows per day (N = sampling machines), so 10 hosts ≈ 28.8k rows/day.
        Derived metrics use Σ (util%/100)×Δt for GPU/CPU hours, Σ (RAM GB)×Δt for GB-hours, and Σ (MB/s × Δt)/1024 for transfer GB.
    </p>
</section>

<section id="machine-detail" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Computer detail</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> per-host drill-down — utilisation, apps, app lists, TUFLOW panel, disk scan, and tracking controls. Open from a hostname on <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="machines">Fleet → Computers</a> or Sessions.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Period</strong> — matches the Computers list range (Today / 24h / 5d / 7d / 30d / All). Stats duration toggle controls how app/process time is shown.</li>
        <li><strong>Utilisation cards</strong> — Active / Passive / Free %; session and hardware breakdowns link from Fleet metric cells.</li>
        <li><strong>Apps grid</strong> — tracked software on this machine; Show Usage Data / Tracking Time Range filters.</li>
        <li><strong>App lists</strong> — apply/remove/customize lists for this host; preferred path for “track TUFLOW here” (not App Lists global apply-to-scope).</li>
        <li><strong>TUFLOW</strong> — recent runs and status when Flood-gated; requires Flood enrollment + updated agent.</li>
        <li><strong>Disk usage scan</strong> — queue a top-folders scan from the machine page; agent reports progress within ~20s poll cadence.</li>
        <li>Links to <a asp-page="/Socratize">Socratize</a>, Stats, and Analyze apps on App Lists.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="user-detail" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">User detail</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> per-person usage — sessions, machines used, hour-of-day / day-of-week patterns. Open by clicking a username on Fleet → Computers or Live.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Period matches the Fleet list you came from.</li>
        <li>Summary cards show active time, machines touched, and dominant apps where tracked.</li>
        <li>Charts highlight when the person tends to be active (hour-of-day, day-of-week).</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="staff-access" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Staff RDP</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> public-team machine pool — book up to 24 hours and Connect via an <code>.rdp</code> file (opens mstsc).</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Sign in with your Windows/AD identity (Negotiate). The pool shows machines whose team is marked <strong>public-facing</strong> under Applications → Teams.</li>
        <li>If you belong to a Remote Access Group, the pool is further limited to those hosts; if you have no RAG membership, you see all public-facing machines.</li>
        <li><strong>Active User</strong> (green) means an Active session is present — you can still book (warning) and Connect (break-glass).</li>
        <li><strong>Book</strong> rejects overlapping bookings; cancel your own booking (admins can cancel any). Duration: 1h / 2h / 4h / 8h / end-of-day / 24h.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect</strong> downloads/opens a tiny <code>.rdp</code> file — browsers cannot launch <code>mstsc</code> directly; use Open with Remote Desktop.</li>
        <li>Live CPU/GPU metrics remain on the per-group Staff page linked as “Live metrics” when you have RAG membership.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="remote-workstations" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Remote Workstations</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> public pool — same machines as Staff RDP, without a sign-in wall.</p>
    <p class="fw-semibold small mb-1">One-click Connect — once per PC</p>
    <ol class="small mb-2">
        <li><strong>Configure</strong> — run the one-time helper (no Heimdall install).</li>
        <li><strong>Connect</strong> — click Connect on a workstation.</li>
        <li>When the browser asks to open an outside application, choose <strong>Always allow</strong>.</li>
        <li>After that, Connect auto-launches Remote Desktop. Windows may warn on the Configure file the first time (More info → Run anyway). Arrow → <em>Download .rdp</em> if you only need a file.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small mb-0">Populate the list under <strong>Applications → Teams</strong>: tick <em>Public-facing</em> and assign machines. Arrow → <em>RDP Settings</em> opens the connection window (display / local resources), then Connect in that app. Search is software/publisher only (partials and wildcards <code>*</code>/<code>?</code>); advertise apps on <a href="#discovery">Discovery</a>, then admins pick which titles show on this page.</p>
</section>

<section id="remote-access-groups" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Remote Access Groups</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> admin setup for <a asp-page="/StaffAccess" asp-fragment="staff-access">Staff Access</a> — who can see which machines, and which processes they can favourite.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Create a group, add staff by email (must match their Windows UPN or <code>user@domain</code> login).</li>
        <li>Assign machines to the group — only those machines are visible to its members.</li>
        <li>Optionally curate a favourite-process shortlist and toggle "favourites only" mode for the group.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="tracking-config" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Tracking config</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> sampling intervals, exclude lists, pauses, and metric thresholds for agents. What to <em>track</em> is owned by <a asp-page="/AppLists">App lists</a>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Configs are scoped (All / Region / Office / Group / Machine) with priority — more specific scopes win.</li>
        <li>Exclude lists and pauses still live here. Prefer App lists (Create → Save → Apply) for includes. Legacy Tracking Config include JSON may still apply until cleared.</li>
        <li>Sample/upload/refresh intervals control agent chattiness — raise them on constrained networks.</li>
        <li>See <a asp-page="/Utilization" asp-fragment="utilization">Utilization criteria</a> for the scoring weights that consume this data.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="auth" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Auth</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> turn team-membership sources on/off under <strong>Admin → Auth</strong>. Not site-wide SSO.
        Flood page allowlists are under <a asp-page="/AccessLists">Admin → Access lists</a>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Manual / CSV</strong> (default on) — backup while Entra permissions are pending. Add people on team detail or <a asp-page="/Teams/ImportCsv">Import CSV</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Entra Graph sync</strong> (default off) — enable after DPAPI secrets are installed and Graph admin consent is granted. Use <em>Test Entra connection</em> on Auth: token OK + group-read fail usually means consent is still missing.</li>
        <li>At least one source stays enabled. Sync from Entra is blocked while the Entra toggle is off.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="access-lists" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Access lists</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2">
        <strong>Purpose:</strong> manage named email allowlists that unlock gated areas of the site
        (<strong>Admin → Access lists</strong>). Site admins only. Saved lists live in the database and apply immediately (no API restart).
        Appsettings values seed each list until you save from the UI.
    </p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Site admins</strong> — read-only here; edit <code>Heimdall:StaffAccess:AdminEmails</code> in API appsettings and restart. Always included in Full Flood.</li>
        <li><strong>Full Flood</strong> — Live, Historical, Enrollment, Run Queue, Fleet Sims, Run behaviour, Flood machine detail, TUFLOW Runs, Machine TUFLOW panel.</li>
        <li><strong>Flood Live only</strong> — Flood → Live tab + shared SSE stream only (no other Flood tabs). Full Flood already includes Live.</li>
        <li>Future page gates can add another named list in the same catalog without a new storage pattern.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="teams" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Teams</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> org teams for Fleet machine sections, people, app lists, and Staff RDP visibility. Under <strong>Applications → Teams</strong>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>The list shows each team’s people, machines, and tracking / ignored app-list counts. Use <strong>Add team</strong> or <strong>Import CSV</strong>; open a row for detail.</li>
        <li>On <strong>Edit team</strong>, set name, code, parent, and <strong>Public-facing</strong> (machines appear in the Staff RDP pool — do not mark Flood/TUFLOW teams public).</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Entra membership</strong> — enable under <a asp-page="/Auth">Admin → Auth</a> when ready.
            On the API host run <code>scripts\Protect-HeimdallEntraSecret.ps1</code>
            (DPAPI file at <code>%ProgramData%\Heimdall\secrets\entra.json</code>).
            Never put <code>ClientSecret</code> in git or <code>appsettings.json</code>.
            App registration needs Application permissions <code>Group.Read.All</code> and <code>User.Read.All</code> (or <code>GroupMember.Read.All</code>) with admin consent; no redirect URI.
            On Edit team, paste the Entra group <strong>Object ID</strong>; on team detail use <em>Sync from Entra</em>
            (username from on-prem SAM when present, else UPN/mail local-part; optional <code>DefaultNetBiosDomain</code>).
        </li>
        <li><strong>Membership</strong> — add/edit/remove people (<code>firstname.lastname</code>); assign or unassign machines (never deletes a machine from the database). Manual people on an Entra-linked team are replaced on the next sync.</li>
        <li><strong>App lists</strong> — link lists as Tracking or Do not track for the whole team; use <strong>Machine overrides</strong> to force track or ignore a list on one host (override wins).</li>
        <li>CSV import supports <code>Username,Team</code> or full columns including <code>ParentTeam</code> and optional <code>Code</code>. Catalog editing stays on <a asp-page="/AppLists">App lists</a>.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="utilization" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Utilization criteria</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> the global weights and targets that produce the <a asp-page="/Socratize" asp-fragment="socratize">Socratize</a> verdict (and utilisation scoring elsewhere).</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Weights (users, daily utilisation, busy metrics, app value) must add to 100% — the page shows a running total.</li>
        <li>Ideal targets (min users, ideal daily %, working hours/day) define what "good" looks like before scoring kicks in.</li>
        <li>High/Adequate/Mixed thresholds decide which verdict bucket a machine lands in.</li>
        <li>License $/year for Socratize is maintained under <a asp-page="/Finance" asp-route-tab="software">Admin → Finance → Software</a> (latest purchase year syncs here).</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="cost" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Cost</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> hardware purchase cost, warranty, support hours, and inventory/reimage identity — under <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="cost">Fleet → Cost</a>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Edit a row to record purchase cost/currency, purchase date, warranty dates, and hardware specs (or let the agent auto-fill what it can detect).</li>
        <li>Support hourly rate feeds into the <a asp-page="/Socratize" asp-fragment="socratize">Socratize</a> brief's cost-per-hour comparison.</li>
        <li>Model-level catalog and $/hour metrics live under <a asp-page="/Finance">Admin → Finance</a> (Fleet → Cost stays the per-machine editor).</li>
        <li>Reimage identity fields help distinguish a genuinely new machine from the same box after a reimage.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="stats" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Stats</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> usage rankings by machine scope — under <a asp-page="/Fleet" asp-route-tab="stats">Fleet → Stats</a>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Pick a scope and date range, then Apply. Rankings appear as cards (Users / Apps / Patterns) — each shows the top 3.</li>
        <li>Click a card header to expand (~10 rows visible, scroll for the rest). Hover the title for what that ranking means.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="site-usage" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Site usage</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Admin → <strong>Site usage</strong> — first-party log of who opened which page, how long they stayed, and which links they clicked (no third-party tracker).</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Page views are recorded server-side; dwell time and link clicks come from a small script in the layout (beacon on unload).</li>
        <li>Identity is best-effort: Windows/Negotiate name when present, else staff cookie email, else <code>anonymous:{session}</code>.</li>
        <li>Toggle with <code>Heimdall:UsageAnalytics:Enabled</code>; prune with <code>RetentionDays</code> (default 90). Export CSV from the Usage page.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="diagnostics" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Diagnostics</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Admin → <strong>Diagnostics</strong> — collect troubleshooting output when the API runs on a remote host (no Cursor machine required).</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Always-on API logs</strong> — <code>%ProgramData%\Heimdall\logs\api\heimdall-api-yyyyMMdd.log</code> (~14 days). First place to look if <code>HeimdallApi</code> will not start.</li>
        <li><strong>Ops log</strong> — <code>%ProgramData%\Heimdall\logs\ops\ops-yyyyMMdd.log</code> records pack, deploy, deposit, restart, cleanup, database-mode switches.</li>
        <li><strong>Install / pack script logs</strong> — same <code>%ProgramData%\Heimdall\logs\</code> tree (<code>install-api-*.log</code>, <code>pack-*.log</code>, Launch Control).</li>
        <li><strong>Collect dump</strong> — Admin → Diagnostics → <strong>Collect diagnostics</strong> writes folder + zip under <code>C:\Temp\Heimdall.API\Logs\diagnostics-{stamp}\</code> (health, services, recent logs, redacted appsettings, Event Log excerpt, DB path/size only — not the DB file). API keys redacted to last 4 chars.</li>
        <li><strong>Service down</strong> — open ProgramData logs + Application Event Log, or run <code>scripts\Collect-Diagnostics.cmd</code> on the API host (same Temp dump root).</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="client-version" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Client Version</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> under <strong>Fleet → Client version</strong> — agent build vs. published version (Client version + Behind), online / in use / <strong>Active User</strong> (green = Active, red = Disconnected), pack readiness, and silent <strong>Deploy Client</strong>.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li>Published version is set automatically when a pack completes (Launch Control or Pack client). Simple integers (baseline <code>3</code> = first UpdateClient-capable). Legacy SemVer reports count as <code>1</code>. Agents below <code>3</code> cannot silent-update — use Launch Control push or Install.lnk once, then Deploy. Use <strong>Save</strong> only to override the baseline.</li>
        <li>On load the page checks source vs pack fingerprints under <code>Heimdall:RepoRoot</code>\<code>dist\Heimdall-Client</code> (same folder Launch Control writes). <strong>Deploy Client</strong> stays locked until the pack is Ready. <strong>Pack client</strong> rebuilds and bumps N+1 only when source changed (or you confirm a forced bump). After Launch Control packs, use <strong>Refresh from disk</strong> (or wait for Setup’s auto-refresh) — do not Pack again just to unlock Deploy.</li>
        <li>Deploy queues a silent agent self-update (download + service restart only). Updates defer while any interactive session is Active — no logoff or reboot. Agents below version <code>3</code> do not support <code>UpdateClient</code> — Deploy marks them Failed with bootstrap guidance.</li>
        <li><strong>Restart agent</strong> — queues <code>RestartAgent</code> (detached <code>sc stop/start HeimdallAgent</code>). <strong>Cleanup staging</strong> — deletes deposit folders only: <code>%ProgramData%\Heimdall\update\</code> (portal Deploy extract under <code>extracted-v{version}-{yyyyMMdd-HHmmss}</code>, plus legacy <code>extracted</code> / <code>extracted-{guid}</code>) and under <code>C:\Temp\</code> any folder named exactly <code>Heimdall-Client</code> or <code>Heimdall-Client-*</code>. Does not touch other Temp content, Program Files Agent, queue, secrets, or logs. Both are blocked while Deploy/UpdateClient is pending; use Cleanup then re-Deploy on hosts stuck with a locked extract folder.</li>
        <li><strong>Deposit pack to Temp</strong> — queues <code>DepositClientPack</code>: the agent downloads the Ready pack from the API into <code>C:\Temp\Heimdall-Client-v{version}-{yyyyMMdd-HHmmss}</code> for manual <code>Install.lnk</code> (does not replace the running agent). Version comes from the Ready pack (<code>PendingClientDeposit</code> / <code>VERSION.json</code>). Pack must be Ready; skipped if Deploy/UpdateClient or Deposit already pending. Launch Control → <strong>Deposit pack via API</strong> uses the same queue (not SMB/robocopy).</li>
        <li><strong>Bootstrap once:</strong> first upgrade from pre-3 / legacy clients needs Heimdall Setup push or <code>Install.lnk</code> from the pack. After that, silent Deploy works for subsequent versions.</li>
        <li>Old <code>/Clients</code> URLs redirect here. Full agent architecture: see <a href="#client-agent">Client / agent</a> or repo doc <code>docs/CLIENT.md</code>.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="client-agent" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Client / agent (how it works)</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> background on the Windows service installed on each workstation — not a dashboard page. Install procedures: <code>INSTALL.md</code> and <code>docs/CLIENT.md</code> in the repo.</p>
    <ol class="small">
        <li><strong>Service</strong> — <code>HeimdallAgent</code> under <code>%ProgramFiles%\Heimdall\Agent\</code>; logs in <code>%ProgramData%\Heimdall\logs\</code>; offline ingest queue <code>queue.db</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Ingest</strong> — every ~60s (configurable): sessions, allowlisted process runs, heartbeat, hardware. Auth: header <code>X-Heimdall-Key</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Config poll</strong> — every ~5 min: merged App lists + Tracking Config + pending commands (Restart RDS, Restart agent, Cleanup staging, Deposit pack, inventory, TUFLOW start, disk scan, UpdateClient).</li>
        <li><strong>Fleet snapshots</strong> — every <strong>30s</strong> for every known machine: CPU/GPU/RAM/disk/network, TUFLOW Y/N, Active/Idle, top processes. Powers Fleet → Live, Computers util columns, and Flood analytics.</li>
        <li><strong>Staff live sampling</strong> — separate 10s loop, only while a Staff/drilldown viewer is active (not the same as fleet snapshots).</li>
        <li><strong>Portable install</strong> — pack once on a build PC (<code>dist\Heimdall-Client\</code>), run <code>Install.lnk</code> on each target (self-contained .NET — no SDK on clients).</li>
        <li><strong>TUFLOW</strong> — Flood-enrolled hosts only; agent includes <code>TuflowLauncher.exe</code>; polls queued starts every ~20s.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-0">
        Troubleshooting: API key mismatch → 401; unreachable API → queue grows; no util data → wait for heartbeat + agent v3+. Prefer Admin → Diagnostics → Collect, or run <code>scripts\Collect-Diagnostics.cmd</code> on the API host (dumps to <code>C:\Temp\Heimdall.API\Logs</code>). Always-on logs: <code>%ProgramData%\Heimdall\logs\api\</code>.
    </p>
</section>

<section id="finance" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Finance</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Admin → <strong>Finance</strong> — hardware model catalog, software license purchases by year, and cost per hour of use for hardware and licenses.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Hardware</strong> — groups machines by brand/model/CPU/GPU/RAM/disk fingerprint; filter by Team (Flood, Energy, Acoustics, …). Edit purchase date, cost, warranty, and specs per host.</li>
        <li><strong>Software</strong> — enter Maker, software name, Discovery process match, license + maintenance cost, year, Design vs Simulation. Latest year per process syncs into Socratize / Utilization license $/year.</li>
        <li><strong>Metrics</strong> — hardware $/user Active hour; Design $/h from ProcessRun time overlapping Active sessions; Simulation $/h from summed run durations (concurrent ≈ multi-seat). Intensity &lt;50% / ≥50% is a separate fit-for-purpose signal.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="admin-preferences" class="hd-panel mb-4 hd-help-section">
    <h2 class="h5">Admin menu: Theme &amp; database mode</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary small mb-2"><strong>Purpose:</strong> personal display preferences and the sandbox/live data toggle, both in the top-right <strong>Admin</strong> menu.</p>
    <ol class="small mb-0">
        <li><strong>Theme</strong> — the Admin menu links to the <a asp-page="/Theme">Theme page</a>, which offers premade looks (Original Recipe, Cosmic, Dark Slate, Light Clean) plus a full colour/font editor for building and saving your own named themes. The active theme is remembered via cookie.</li>
        <li><strong>Database mode</strong> — <em>Live</em> shows real agent data; <em>Sandbox (DEV)</em> is an isolated demo/test database. Agent ingest always writes to Live regardless of which mode you're browsing — don't confuse a quiet Sandbox view with agents being down.</li>
        <li>A <span class="hd-dev-badge" style="font-size:0.7rem;padding:0.1rem 0.4rem;">DEV</span>-style badge in the header means you're currently viewing the sandbox, not production data.</li>
    </ol>
</section>
